WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.310
This video contains spoilers for the final
season of Game of Thrones - but according

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.910
to critics the show isn't just spoiled already,
it's become downright rancid.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.049
Yes, the latest episode of Game of Thrones
has been getting the worst reviews in the

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:18.070
show's eight season history, as fans and critics
alike have banded together to condemn the

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:23.310
decision to have fan-favorite Daenerys Targaryen
suddenly go completely mental and murder tens

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.840
of thousands of people for no good reason.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.369
Titled "The Bells," the fifth episode of season
8 has a dire 47% fresh rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.780
That's several percentage points lower than
the previous worst-reviewed episode, "Unbowed,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.489
Unbent, and Unbroken" from way back in season
5.

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:44.610
And it's just the latest blow in the show's
relentless descent into disappointment.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:49.520
Every episode so far this season has had worse
reviews than the previous episode, dropping

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.039
from 92% for the season premiere... to 88%
for episode 2...

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:58.609
75% for episode 3... and 57% for episode 4.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:00:59.809
How bad is it?

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:05.680
The season as a whole currently sits at 71%
fresh, which sounds decent until you realize

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.070
that the lowest total rating for any of the
previous seven seasons was 91% for the very

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.130
first season.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.000
And those are just the critics.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.860
Fans have been even harsher, giving this season
just a 43% rating so far.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:19.909
Ouch!

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:24.520
Critics didn't pull any punches in their reviews,
with much of the disdain aimed at the showrunners.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.490
Maureen Ryan of the Hollywood Reporter wrote
that she was shouting "don't do it!" at the

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.490
TV screen.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.950
"I wasn't talking to Daenerys.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.229
I was shouting at the writers and architects
of Game of Thrones, executive producers David

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.299
Benioff and D.B. Weiss.

00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:38.410
But they did it.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.490
They not only gave Daenerys the least-earned
character turn in the show's history - maybe

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.060
in television history - they rubbed our faces
in this colossal mistake for what felt like

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.130
an eternity.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:54.000
To say the show befouled the bed in its penultimate
episode, 'The Bells,' is putting it mildly."

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.000
She continued,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
"'The Bells' was Game of Thrones at its worst,
and it did untold damage to the show as a

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.000
whole.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.659
It's going to be hard to think of the show
without feeling nauseated by what it did - especially

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:06.799
to its women - in the home stretch.

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:11.250
[...] Inescapably, infuriatingly, what we're
left with is apparently the central message

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.490
of Game of Thrones: B------ are crazy."

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.580
Anna Leszkiewicz of New Statesman agreed.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:22.340
"'She went mad' feels like a lazy, stereotypical,
unearned plot device and one that was truly

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.050
horrible to watch.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.230
At times, the massacre felt like pointless,
gratuitous audience torture … It feels like

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:33.550
Game of Thrones is throwing a major character
under the bus for the sake of an easy ending."

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:37.120
Akhil Arora of Gadgets360 had similar thoughts:

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.130
"Despite having some of the most well-acted
moments and epic scenes in its roster, Game

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.810
of Thrones season 8 episode 5 ultimately felt
hollow.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.010
This used to be a show where major points
of a character's arc were laid atop on seasons

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:50.340
of foundation.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.400
But in its most pressing hour, Benioff and
Weiss have largely thrown all that out of

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.260
the window in service of shock and moving
onto the next story beat."

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.480
And The Daily Beast's Melissa Leon wrote,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.530
"The Mother of Dragons becoming a 'Mad Queen'
may work on paper with what the show has foreshadowed

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.840
for seasons all this talk of Targaryens' hereditary
madness, and her own streak of cruelty with

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.560
her enemies but her split-second decision
to systematically murder half a city of innocent

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.310
people does not."

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.000
Vulture's Kathryn VanArendonk drove the point
home, writing

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.060
"Disheartened isn’t even the right word.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.910
I am exhausted by Mad Queen Dany [...] The
notion that this sprawling story about epic

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:32.030
political gamesmanship, the corruption of
power, the things we inherit from our families,

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.560
the people we choose to love, and the inevitable
inescapable march of death would ultimately

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.870
hinge on a trope as painfully stale as 'and
then the scary powerful woman goes crazy'

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.480
is, to be frank, boring."

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.090
NPR's Glen Weldon was more succinct:

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:48.800
"Dany got a raw deal.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.080
Narratively speaking, Game of Thrones did
the Mother of Dragons dirty."

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.080
So can the show redeem itself in the series
finale?

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.390
After what happened in "The Bells," it's hard
to see how.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.370
As the BBC's Hugh Montgomery wrote,

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:05.370
"By the end of this 90 minutes, as the ash
settled, it is difficult not to feel one was

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:09.390
looking at the charred remains of an era-defining
television show's integrity."

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.090
"Dracarys."

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.790
Rest in peace, Game of Thrones.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.320
Rest in peace.

